WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.400
Who decides what each account may do,
and who checks?

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.780
IAM goes wrong in four ways.

00:00:07.117 --> 00:00:09.127
First, the account nobody owns.

00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:12.207
Most identities in a
company are not people.

00:00:12.487 --> 00:00:14.957
They are service accounts,
keys and scripts.

00:00:15.307 --> 00:00:18.897
Their creator left years ago,
and their passwords never change.

00:00:19.232 --> 00:00:21.342
Their rights were set generously, once.

00:00:21.692 --> 00:00:24.002
Nobody notices when one is abused.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:27.420
Second, roles designed too wide.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.430
A role gets a wildcard,
because the precise list was long.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.960
Then the role is copied to the next team.

00:00:34.235 --> 00:00:39.265
Years later, one stolen developer
laptop equals the entire data estate.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.710
Nobody remembers deciding it.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.100
Third, one person holds both signatures.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.330
Creating a supplier is fine.

00:00:47.655 --> 00:00:49.365
Releasing a payment is fine.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.520
The same account doing both means one
attacker can invent a vendor and pay it.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.660
Both actions are logged,
and both look legitimate.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.020
Fourth, the login everybody shares.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.410
A team of four uses one
administrator account.

00:01:04.775 --> 00:01:08.425
The password lives in a spreadsheet,
and nobody changes it.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:09.780
When something disappears,

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.500
the log names admin.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.290
And last summer's intern can still log in.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.460
What good looks like.

00:01:16.745 --> 00:01:19.775
An inventory gives every
account a named owner.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.610
A vault holds the secrets.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:25.510
Roles are built from what the job does,
with no wildcards.

00:01:25.895 --> 00:01:30.045
A written matrix says which
permissions no single account may hold.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.450
Only personal accounts, with
privileged access checked out per task.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:38.540
Business owners, not IT,
confirm access every quarter.

00:01:38.845 --> 00:01:43.215
NIS2 requires access control policies,
in Article 21.

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:46.273
A suggested first step.

00:01:46.558 --> 00:01:47.828
Ask two questions.

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.143
Who owns the list of who may do what?

00:01:50.418 --> 00:01:52.768
And when did a business
owner last sign it off?

00:01:53.058 --> 00:01:57.108
If either answer is a shrug, the rights
are whatever they have grown into.

00:01:58.683 --> 00:02:00.333
Four ways rights go wrong.

00:02:00.633 --> 00:02:03.283
Name an owner,
and have the business sign it off.

